  "faqs": [
   {
    "q": "Should I muzzle my dog for grooming?",
    "a": "A muzzle can make grooming safer if your dog might bite. But train your dog to wear it comfortably first, using treats and gradual steps, rather than putting it on for the first time during a stressful session. A basket muzzle lets your dog pant, drink and take treats. A muzzle is a safety tool. It doesn't make grooming less frightening, so keep working on the fear itself."
   },
   {
    "q": "How long does it take to fix grooming fear?",
    "a": "It depends on the dog and the fear, so there's no fixed timeline. What matters is moving at your dog's pace: only take the next step when your dog is relaxed and happy with the current one. If you're not seeing progress, or your dog's fear is severe, get help from a qualified behavior professional."
   },
   {
    "q": "My dog is fine with me but bites the groomer. Why?",
    "a": "Being handled by a stranger, or handled in unfamiliar ways, can make even a friendly dog more likely to growl or bite. Tell the groomer about it before the next appointment. Consider muzzle training, and practice gentle handling at home. If the biting continues, talk to your vet and a qualified behavior professional."
   }
  ],
