  "faqs": [
   {
    "q": "My dog has scabs after a haircut. Is that normal?",
    "a": "Scabs mean the skin was broken or irritated, so they're worth a vet check rather than waiting: Possible causes: a hot or damaged blade, a nick, or scratching at irritated skin. Infection: less often, scabs and sores after grooming are a sign of a skin infection that needs prompt treatment. See your vet the same day if the scabs are spreading, painful, or your dog seems unwell."
   },
   {
    "q": "Can I put coconut oil on my dog's dry skin?",
    "a": "Ask your vet before putting anything on your dog's skin. Dry, flaky skin has many possible causes, and the right approach depends on the cause."
   },
   {
    "q": "Can I treat a hot spot at home?",
    "a": "No, see your vet. A hot spot is a painful, inflamed, often oozing patch that can spread quickly. Waiting for it to clear up on its own tends to make it worse, and your vet will need to find what's causing the itching."
   }
  ],
